Container always contains only one child
because is designed as single child widget


The simplest way to remember it is:

Column = arrange things from TOP → BOTTOM
Row = arrange things from LEFT → RIGHT


1. Use Column when things are vertical ↓
Column(
  children: [
    Text("Google Notes"),
    Text("My Notes"),
    Icon(Icons.note),
  ],
)

UI:

Google Notes
     ↓
My Notes
     ↓
   📝

For your Google Notes app, you might use a Column for the overall screen:

Column(
  children: [
    SearchBar(),
    NotesList(),
    BottomButton(),
  ],
)


2. Use Row when things are horizontal →
Row(
  children: [
    Icon(Icons.menu),
    Text("Google Notes"),
    Icon(Icons.search),
  ],
)

UI:

☰    Google Notes    🔍

This is perfect for your top app bar.


Expanded tells a Row or Column: "Give this widget all the remaining available space


1--> Overlay
overlayColor controls the temporary color shown when the user presses, hovers over, or interacts with the button.

resolveWith allows you to decide what color to use depending on the button's current state.

WidgetStateProperty.all(...) means you're giving Flutter one shape to use for all states.